,1\r\n432,QUICK ONLINE TRANSFER,,1\r\n431,CONTACTLESS PAYMENT,A BLIK functionality used for making payments in physical stores without a code,1\r\n433,CZYŻAK,Czyżak is an author mentioned in the context of discussing cybercrime in mobile banking,1\r\n303,PAY-BY-LINK,,3\r\n299,COVID-19 PANDEMIC,\"The COVID-19 pandemic, a global health crisis that began in 2020, had profound and far-reaching impacts on various aspects of life worldwide, including in Poland. It led to the declaration of a state of epidemic in Poland, prompting significant administrative changes across multiple sectors. One of the most notable effects of the pandemic was its influence on consumer behavior and payment trends. As physical trade faced restrictions, there was a marked increase in online transactions and e-commerce activities. This shift was accompanied by a growing preference for cashless transactions, as consumers and businesses alike sought safer and more convenient payment methods.\n\nIn Poland, the pandemic accelerated the adoption of digital payment systems, such as BLIK, and increased the popularity of online shopping. The demand for payment cards surged as consumers adapted to new shopping habits. Additionally, the pandemic spurred a significant rise in mobile banking usage, particularly among older generations who had previously been less inclined to use digital financial services. This shift was partly driven by the necessity to minimize physical contact and adhere to social distancing measures.\n\nThe retail and service sectors in Poland also underwent administrative changes, with a strong emphasis on promoting cashless payments to reduce the risk of virus transmission. These changes were part of broader efforts to adapt to the new normal and ensure the safety of both consumers and employees.\n\nOverall, the COVID-19 pandemic acted as a catalyst for digital transformation in Poland's financial landscape, influencing consumer behavior and accelerating the adoption of cashless and online payment systems. The increased use of mobile banking and payment platforms like BLIK among all age groups, including the oldest consumers, highlights the pandemic's role in reshaping financial habits and preferences.\",17\r\n317,MASTERCARD,\"Mastercard is a global payment technology company that has been proactive in adapting to the evolving needs of its users, particularly during the COVID-19 pandemic. In response to the increased demand for safer and more convenient payment methods, Mastercard increased the contactless transaction limit. This strategic move was aimed at reducing the need for PIN entry, thereby minimizing physical contact and enhancing the safety and convenience of transactions during the pandemic.\n\nIn addition to its efforts to improve transaction safety, Mastercard has also been active in expanding its technological capabilities through strategic partnerships. In 2019, Mastercard partnered with PSP to enhance BLIK's contactless payment capabilities. This collaboration was part of Mastercard's broader strategy to innovate and improve the efficiency and reach of its payment solutions, ensuring that users have access to seamless and advanced payment technologies.\n\nThrough these initiatives, Mastercard has demonstrated its commitment to providing secure, efficient, and innovative payment solutions on a global scale.\",2\r\n315,MINISTRY OF DEVELOPMENT,The Ministry of Development issued recommendations to limit COVID-19 transmission in retail outlets,1\r\n316,VISA,Visa increased the contactless transaction limit to reduce the need for PIN entry during the COVID-19 pandemic,1\r\n322,ZWIĄZEK PRZEDSIĘBIORCÓW I PRACODAWCÓW,,1\r\n321,FORBES,Forbes is a media organization that reported on the increase of the contactless transaction limit during the COVID-19 pandemic,1\r\n50,ING BANK ŚLĄSKI,\"ING Bank Śląski is a prominent financial institution in Poland, recognized for its leadership in mobile banking services. Established as one of the top five banks in the country by client numbers, ING Bank Śląski has consistently demonstrated innovation in the digital banking sector. The bank was a pioneer in offering mobile banking applications, launching its first app in 2012. It was notably the first Polish bank to introduce a mobile banking application specifically designed for tablets, showcasing its commitment to technological advancement and customer convenience.\n\nA key player in the development of mobile payment systems, ING Bank Śląski was one of the six banks involved in the creation of the BLIK payment system, which has become a widely used service in Poland. Since 2015, the bank has been providing BLIK services to its customers, enabling them to perform contactless payments and other functionalities with ease. In addition to BLIK, ING Bank Śląski offers a range of mobile payment options, including HCE, Google Pay, Apple Pay, and Garmin Pay, catering to the diverse needs of its tech-savvy clientele.\n\nThe bank's involvement in the Polish Standard of Payments further underscores its role in shaping the country's digital payment landscape. With a significant number of mobile banking users and high mobilization indicators, ING Bank Śląski continues to lead in the adoption and implementation of cutting-edge banking technologies. Its comprehensive suite of mobile banking services not only enhances customer experience but also solidifies its position as a forward-thinking institution in the competitive Polish banking market.\",7\r\n60,POLSKI STANDARD PŁATNOŚCI,\"Polski Standard Płatności is a company that was established in 2013 by a consortium of six banks with the primary objective of managing and coordinating the development of the BLIK mobile payment system in Poland. As the entity responsible for overseeing the BLIK project, Polski Standard Płatności plays a crucial role in the advancement and management of this innovative payment system within the country. The BLIK system, under the stewardship of Polski Standard Płatności, has become a significant player in the Polish financial landscape, offering a convenient and secure mobile payment solution to users. Through its efforts, Polski Standard Płatności continues to enhance the functionality and reach of BLIK, ensuring it meets the evolving needs of consumers and remains at the forefront of mobile payment technology in Poland.\",7\r\n59,PKO BP,\"PKO BP, also known as IKO, is a prominent financial institution in Poland, recognized for its extensive mobile banking services. As one of the six banks involved in the creation of the BLIK payment system, PKO BP has been offering BLIK services since 2015, which include contactless payments and other functionalities. The bank is a key player in the Polish Standard of Payments and has introduced the IKO mobile payment system, further solidifying its position in the mobile banking sector.\n\nWith a strong focus on digital innovation, PKO BP has developed a robust mobile banking platform that caters to a large customer base. The bank boasts 3.91 million active mobile app users and serves a total of 10.54 million individual clients. This highlights its significant presence in the Polish banking industry and its commitment to providing convenient and efficient banking solutions to its customers.\n\nPKO BP's mobile application usage metrics have been analyzed, indicating its role as a major bank in Poland with moderate mobile banking indicators. The bank's mobile services are well-regarded, and it continues to enhance its offerings to meet the evolving needs of its clients. As a leading bank in Poland, PKO BP remains dedicated to delivering high-quality financial services and maintaining its reputation as a trusted and innovative banking institution.\",6\r\n288,SANTANDER BANK,\"Santander Bank, formerly known as Bank Zachodni WBK, is one of the largest banks in Poland. It is renowned for its substantial client base and leadership in mobile banking services. As a major financial institut